[{"source" ":org/Math-Academy" "forms" [{:db/id 17592186162009 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162636 :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{2}$ are negative semi-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=-1$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn.\n\n- Quadratic form I is *not* negative semi-definite since some of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are positive (for example, ${\\lambda }_{1}=3$ is positive).\n\n- Quadratic form II is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are non-positive.\n\n- Quadratic form III is negative semi-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are non-positive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186161108 :answer-field/correct 17592186161113 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/id #uuid "aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :question/math-academy-id "q-94788" :question/problem "Which of the following quadratic forms over ${R}^{3}$ are negative-definite?\n\nI. ${x}^{T}\\,Qx,$ where the eigenvalues of $Q$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}={\\lambda }_{2}=-3$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-9$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,Rx,$ where the eigenvalues of $R$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=0,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-5$\n\nIII. ${x}^{T}\\,Sx,$ where the eigenvalues of $S$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=-6,$ ${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ and ${\\lambda }_{3}=-3$" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our quadratic forms in turn. \n\n- Quadratic form I is negative-definite. Indeed, all of the eigenvalues of $Q$ are negative.\n\n- Quadratic form II is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $R$ are negative (for example, ${\\lambda }_{2}=5$ is not negative).\n\n- Quadratic form III is *not* negative-definite since not all of the eigenvalues of $S$ are negative (${\\lambda }_{2}=10$ is not negative).\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/id #uuid "feddc0fa-b88d-5ca9-bd43-99d017ae0768" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" "answer-2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" "answer-1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" "answer-bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/id #uuid "2274a1ba-cb09-551d-a264-6a5d4c0e37fd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/id #uuid "2dffc13b-109f-56f9-902b-d52e5de34dcf" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/id #uuid "12d6028b-8c3e-5aba-826a-b147b99e2f9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/id #uuid "1b4abab5-df3e-5347-99fb-84fa6de03a5e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/id #uuid "bca55a76-d282-5f41-9c77-abe46819afe7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/id #uuid "95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :question/math-academy-id "q-94796" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The eigenvalues of $A$ are ${\\lambda }_{1}=4$ and ${\\lambda }_{2}=6$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Given a quadratic form $Q(x)={x}^{T}Ax,$ we have the following theorem:\n\n- $Q$ is positive-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are positive,\n\n- $Q$ is positive semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-negative (note that positive semi-definite quadratic forms include all positive-definite quadratic forms),\n\n- $Q$ is negative-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative,\n\n- $Q$ is negative semi-definite if and only if all the eigenvalues of $A$ are non-positive (note that negative semi-definite quadratic forms include all negative-definite quadratic forms), and\n\n- $Q$ is indefinite if and only if $A$ has both positive and negative eigenvalues.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the characteristic equation of $A,$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|A-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}-5-\\lambda  & 1 \\\\ 1 & -5-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (-5-\\lambda )(-5-\\lambda )-1 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+10\\lambda +24 & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =-6,\\,-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Since all the eigenvalues of $A$ are negative, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/id #uuid "fc3a16ca-ab9d-5910-8403-1994cf7ec17a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" "answer-4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" "answer-df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" "answer-342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/id #uuid "a5a717d0-ad06-5979-9d2e-d3fb8cedc6ac" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/id #uuid "4c8fe2d0-662b-51dd-836f-b0095a0df130" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/id #uuid "df3ce743-bd9f-55c4-aa50-daa5ef48006d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/id #uuid "34e801a5-3875-5ecd-b05f-15545a5c3b9f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/id #uuid "342fba80-10c2-55b1-8abf-7ff9c9e264ed" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186162010 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/id #uuid "7aa930b4-3c77-5008-aaf5-9bbf298703c7" :question/math-academy-id "q-94800" :question/problem "Consider the matrix $A=[\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}].$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${a}_{11}=-7$ and $det(A)=-3$\n\nII. ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ is negative-definite\n\nIII. $A$ is positive-definite" :question/worked-solution "Let ${Δ}_{i}$ for $i=1,2,…,n$  denote the leading principal minors of an $n\\times n$ matrix $A.$ Then, according to Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x$ (as well as the matrix $A$) is \n\n- positive-definite if and only if ${Δ}_{i}>0$ for all $i=1,2,…,n,$\n\n- negative-definite if and only if, for all $i=1,2,…,n,$ we have ${Δ}_{i}<0$ when $i$ is odd, and ${Δ}_{i}>0$ when $i$ is even.\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Indeed, ${a}_{11}=-7$ and \n\n$$\ndet(A)=|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true, while statement III is false. Notice that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{Δ}_{1} & ={a}_{11}=-7<0, \\\\ {Δ}_{2} & =|\\begin{aligned}-7 & 2 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}|=3>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, by Sylvester's criterion, the quadratic form ${x}^{T}\\,A\\,x,$ and the matrix $A,$ are negative-definite.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/id #uuid "079db36a-f121-5453-be8e-d40d62051237" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" "answer-3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" "answer-1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" "answer-5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/id #uuid "a1ff6a24-e096-5a38-adf3-8f6baa63dc90" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/id #uuid "3543723c-355b-52c7-b10b-104509d59ebd" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/id #uuid "1c770309-60dd-57d9-8388-da614d588229" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/id #uuid "f936acee-b9ba-5a2d-aab8-0d02b6a64ffc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/id #uuid "5585210e-e5b7-562b-96ab-a025b6173eec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "activity-5dca4ae1-f844-539e-b380-ba8dbc5f5355" :activity/id #uuid "5dca4ae1-f844-539e-b380-ba8dbc5f5355" :activity/title "Positive-Definite and Negative-Definite Quadratic Forms" :activity/type :activity.type/review :activity/scope 17592186048555 :activity/course 17592186046658 :activity/steps ["step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" "step-014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2"] :activity/first-step "step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :db/ensure [:activity/validate]} {:db/id "step-a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :step/id #uuid "a02e4cde-95d3-5443-90f2-5b20307605ab" :step/content 17592186162636 :step/next "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :step/id #uuid "561ae071-c31b-5dd5-8e25-204838ce97f8" :step/content 17592186162638 :step/next "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :step/id #uuid "06a5f906-8b72-514b-9062-d1bf33299610" :step/content "question-aa5d7a51-2b25-530e-bc34-f2b29d7db7d9" :step/next "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "step-5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :step/id #uuid "5937adbb-f9b5-52b1-9c19-002f11b099e9" :step/content "question-95072baf-8f6f-5ac9-b278-bac347e84d65" :step/next "step-014ca587-44a7-5c4b-a2b6-7b58d26e96d2" :db/ensure [:step/validate]} {:db/id "